  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nThe core idea is to transform the discrete overlap minimization problem into a continuous optimization task by exploiting the periodic structure suggested by both the problem's symmetry and the current best solution. This approach leverages the Fourier basis to represent the step function, using Gershgorin circle theorem-inspired projections to enforce sparsity, and formulates the optimization as a semi-definite program with rank-one constraints. The method systematically reduces the search space through geometric constraints and adaptively adjusts the number of periods while maintaining the required integral constraint.\n\nThe primary bottleneck addressed is the non-convex optimization challenge. Previous approaches used direct gradient-based methods that get stuck in local optima. This strategy uses a combination of:\n- Geometric constraints to bound the function's variations\n- Fourier regularization to capture periodic patterns\n- Semi-definite programming to ensure orthogonality in the solution space\n- Progressive dimensionality reduction from coarse to fine grids\n\nThe approach is fundamentally different from previous hill-climbing methods by:\n- Constructing a complete search trajectory\n- Maintaining exact relationships between periods\n- Using certified dual bounds for comparison\n- Defining fitness through alternative diverging metrics\n\nImportant limitations include:\n- Fixed representation using Fourier basis\n- Requires the current period (p=16) to be a divisor\n- Theoretical minimum suggests C<0.38085\n- Proof sketch incomplete\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\nThe step function h(x): [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] is expressed in Fourier basis:\n\nh(x) = (1/2) + \u2211_{k=1}^{K} a_k cos(2\u03c0 k x / P) + b_k sin(2\u03c0 k x / P)\n\nWith period P=16 (if not already periodic). The integral constraint is:\n\n\u222b_0^2 h(x) dx = 1   \u2192   \u2211_{i=0}^{511} h[i] = 256 (dx=2/512)\n\nThe C5 objective is:\n\nC5 = (2/512) * max_{k} |\u2211_{i} h[i] * (1-h[i+k])|  (discrete convolution)\n\nFormulated as a quadratic program:\n\nmin_{a,b} {max_z} s.t.\n- Orthogonality: \u2211_{k} a_k^2 + b_k^2 \u2264 T (clipping threshold)\n- Depth control: a_{k}^2/b_{k}^2 \u2265 \u03bc (simplification ratio)\n- Integral constraint: \u2211_{n} (1/2) + ... = 256\n- Sparsity control: (a_k / a_{k-1}) \u2264 \u03bb (geometric progression)\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n1. Initialize with parent's solution if available, else:\n   base = np.random.rand(parent_n) / 2\n\n2. Compute discrete Fourier transform:\n   dh = h_base * dx -> re-normalize\n\n3. Hamiltonian simulation:\n   Create coefficient syzygy matrix H:\n        H = I + exp(-\u03b1|dy|) .* (integral matrix)\n\n4. Gradient ascent for worst-case shift:\n   Let W = {k: correlation_coefficients}\n   \u2207h = h * 1-h  (worst-case shift derivative)\n\n   Use exponential step:\n\n   h_new = h_old - \u03bc \u2207h\n\n   then clip to [0,1] and project_vector to enforce integral=1\n\n5. Dimensionality reduction pass:\n   while improvement_threshold and time:\n        n_periods = round(16 / p_i)\n        rebuild_vectors(m) = m'''...(nested)''' with p_i replaced\n\n6. Secondary objective:\n   corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")\n   C5 = dx * max(corr)\n   enforce_vector(h) normalize to sum=256\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\n- Period adjustments: start with p_base=16, then expand to p / 2^m\n- Coefficient arrangements:\n  basic: equilibrium_ratio=0.7, \n  variant: sparsity=min(0.6, 1-loss)\n  alternate: frequency modulation by sin\n   \n- Gradient settings:\n  learning_rate = 0.01 / (1+f_loss)\n  adaptive_loss_margin = 0.001 if flat else 0\n\n- Triggered adjustments:\n  when stall_count > 100 and improvement_slope < 0.005:\n      p_base *= 0.7\n      re_training(n_sweep=5)\n\n5. Computational budget and robustness:\n- Phase1 (15% budget): coarse period search (n=128, 300s)\n- Phase2 (50% budget): fine search (n=256, 400s) [timer interrupt]\n- Phase3 (35% budget): resolution push (n=512, 220s)\n\nFallback: aliases_transplant(source, target_scale)\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n1. Compute cheat-sheet from scratch:\n   integral = np.sum(h) * dx\n   check against 1.0\n\n2. Brand new C5 computation with parent's evaluator\n\n3. Resampling validation:\n   repeats = 5\n   corr_history = [compute_c5(h_repeated) for _ in range(repeats)]\n\n4. Local consistency check:\n   L_infinity = max - min difference <= 0.0001\n\n5. Relative tolerance: if C5 < 10 epsilon, accept\n\nOutput: \n   (h_list[0].tolist(), float(best_c5),\n   int(n_periods * base_resolution))",
